  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which value of $k$ gives precisely one solution to the equation ${x}^{2}-1=k?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =k \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{k+1} \\\\ |x| & =\\sqrt{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation has only one solution if the right-hand side is equal to zero, and the right-hand side is zero only if the expression underneath the radical is zero. So, we solve for the value of $k$ that makes the expression under the radical equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =0 \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-1$ will give only one solution to the equation.",
